If the principal filter of a singleton {n} in the flat domain lies below an element, then the element equals that principal filter.

Classification says the element is the bottom element or an atom {m}. If it is the bottom element, membership of {n} in the bottom element rewrites as membership in the master neighbourhood, so n + 1 lies in {n}. If it is the atom {m}, the inequality of principal filters gives {m} contained in {n}, so m equals n.
